      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 International Society for Sexual Medicine.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Besides lifelong premature ejaculation (LPE) and acquired premature ejaculation (APE), Waldinger and Schweitzer proposed two addition PE syndromes (variable PE [VPE] and subjective PE [SPE])."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "We assessed the associations between intravaginal ejaculatory latency time (IELT) and National Institutes of Health-Chronic Prostatitis Symptom Index (NIH-CPSI) in men with different PE syndromes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "From September 2011 to September 2012, a total of 4,000 men were enrolled from the Anhui province of China. Subjects were required to complete a verbal questionnaire, including demographic information, medical and sexual history (e.g., IELT), and self-estimated scales (e.g., NIH-CPSI)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "IELT; NIH-CPSI; the new classification of PE syndromes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Of 3,016 of the men evaluated, 25.80% complained of PE. Distribution of the four PE syndromes among men with complaints of PE was as follows: LPE, 12.34%; APE, 18.77%; VPE, 44.09%; and SPE, 24.81%. Men with complaints of PE reported worse NIH-CPSI scores and lower IELT than men without complaints of PE (P\u2009<\u20090.001 for all). Moreover, total and subdomain scores of NIH-CPSI were higher in men with APE, and IELT was higher in men with SPE. IELT was negatively associated with NIH-CPSI scores in men with complaints of PE. Negative relationships between total and subdomain scores of NIH-CPSI and IELT were stronger in men with APE (total scores: adjusted r\u2009=\u2009-0.68, P\u2009<\u20090.001; pain symptoms: adjusted r\u2009=\u2009-0.70, P\u2009<\u20090.001; urinary symptoms: adjusted r\u2009=\u2009-0.67, P\u2009<\u20090.001; quality of life impact: adjusted r\u2009=\u2009-0.64, P\u2009<\u20090.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Men with complaints of PE reported worse NIH-CPSI scores than men without complaints of PE. Relationships between IELT and NIH-CPSI scores were strongest in men with APE."
          }
        ]
      },
